    <div class="tab-panel" id="panel-url-schema"><div class="sumcard done"><div class="sumcard-icon">🧩</div><div class="sumcard-body"><div class="sumcard-head"><span class="pill done">Done</span><strong>Structured data — now matches or exceeds WordPress</strong></div><div class="sumcard-text">Structured data coverage went from a 3.3% starting point (18 pages) to effectively complete: 181 static pages were given WebPage + BreadcrumbList JSON-LD via the Pages API (taking coverage to 36.2%, 199 pages); a precise 3-character bug that had been silently breaking JSON-LD on all 242 blog posts was traced to the page template's freeform code field and fixed, bringing 241 of 242 posts to fully valid schema (80.0% sitewide, 440 pages); and on 2026-07-20, <code>/gum-disease</code>, <code>/periodontists</code> and all 97 <code>/london/*</code> location pages were each given a <code>Dentist</code> local-business node carrying the clinic's real name, address and phone number, plus a page-specific <code>areaServed</code> value — verified with no duplicate schema nodes created anywhere. Separately, the 4 utility "Thank You" form pages were given WebPage + BreadcrumbList JSON-LD (4/4 fixed, 0 pending). A full live re-crawl of every sitemap-indexed EN page (553 URLs, 21 July 2026) confirmed <strong>553/553 (100%) render at least one valid JSON-LD block live</strong> — via either the page-level Pages API field or the sitewide schema script, whichever applies per page. Two pages that sit outside the sitemap by design (<code>/london-dentists</code>, <code>/dental-implant-brands</code>) were separately found with no schema at all; both were given WebPage + BreadcrumbList JSON-LD and published live on 21 July 2026, verified with a fresh crawl of each page. No known schema gaps remain anywhere on the site. The earlier mid-way snapshot (99.6%, 553/555) is superseded by this fresh crawl. Homepage/About schema also had a wrong phone number caught and corrected to the clinic's real number, <code>020 3583 9809</code>. Three legacy custom-code scripts with duplicate/stale schema (<code>DentistSchemaJSONLD</code>, <code>MaidaDentistSchema</code>, <code>jsonldlive</code>) still need to be deleted directly in Site Settings &rarr; Custom Code — this method is confirmed blocked from doing this (HTTP 400 on every attempt).</div></div></div>
<div class="sumcard done"><div class="sumcard-icon">🏷️</div><div class="sumcard-body"><div class="sumcard-head"><span class="pill done">Verified live, 22 Jul 2026</span><strong>Schema.org types actually used on the site, by page type</strong></div><div class="sumcard-text">Directly inspected the live JSON-LD on a representative sample (homepage, a treatment hub, a location page, a team bio page, a blog post, and a utility page) rather than relying on names from memory. The schema.org <code>@type</code> values found in use:
<table style="margin-top:10px;"><thead><tr><th>Page type</th><th>Schema types present</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Homepage</td><td><code>Dentist</code>, <code>MedicalOrganization</code>, <code>WebPage</code>, <code>BreadcrumbList</code>, <code>ListItem</code>, <code>Review</code>, <code>Rating</code>, <code>AggregateRating</code>, <code>Person</code>, <code>PostalAddress</code>, <code>GeoCoordinates</code>, <code>ContactPoint</code>, <code>OpeningHoursSpecification</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Treatment hub pages (e.g. <code>/general-dentists</code>)</td><td><code>WebPage</code>, <code>BreadcrumbList</code>, <code>ListItem</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>London location pages (e.g. <code>/london/chelsea</code>)</td><td><code>Dentist</code>, <code>Place</code>, <code>PostalAddress</code>, <code>WebPage</code>, <code>WebSite</code>, <code>BreadcrumbList</code>, <code>ListItem</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Team bio pages (e.g. <code>/team/dr-pedro-gutierres</code>)</td><td><code>Dentist</code>, <code>Person</code>, <code>ImageObject</code>, <code>WebPage</code>, <code>BreadcrumbList</code>, <code>ListItem</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Blog posts (e.g. <code>/blog/dental-journey</code>)</td><td><code>BlogPosting</code>, <code>Organization</code>, <code>ImageObject</code>, <code>WebPage</code>, <code>BreadcrumbList</code>, <code>ListItem</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Utility pages (e.g. <code>/price-list</code>)</td><td><code>WebPage</code>, <code>WebSite</code>, <code>BreadcrumbList</code>, <code>ListItem</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
The <code>Dentist</code> type (a subtype of <code>LocalBusiness</code>/<code>MedicalBusiness</code>) is the core local-SEO signal, correctly present on the homepage, every location page, and every team member's own bio page — each carrying the clinic's real name, address, and phone number. <code>BreadcrumbList</code> and <code>WebPage</code> are the universal baseline present on effectively every page type. Blog posts additionally carry <code>BlogPosting</code> for article-rich-result eligibility.</div></div></div>
<div class="sumcard pending"><div class="sumcard-icon">📋</div><div class="sumcard-body"><div class="sumcard-head"><span class="pill pending">Scoped, not built — awaiting a decision</span><strong>FAQPage schema — a real opportunity, but not a safe blanket win</strong></div><div class="sumcard-text">Investigated adding <code>FAQPage</code>/<code>Question</code>/<code>Answer</code> schema to capture rich-result real estate for the site's FAQ content. Found it's not a mechanical win: the visible "FAQ" accordion on treatment pages (e.g. dental crowns) is actually a type/category list ("Metal crowns", "Porcelain crowns"), not real questions, and the dedicated <strong>FAQ Items CMS collection (419 items total) is mixed quality</strong> — filtering the full set found <strong>172 genuine questions</strong> ("Can dental implants be placed at the same time as extraction?") versus <strong>247 topic labels</strong> ("Routine exams", "Benefits of composite veneers") that would misuse the schema type if marked up as Q&amp;A, risking a Google manual action for spammy structured data. <strong>Client decision needed on implementation approach</strong> before building: (1) add a new CMS field per relevant collection and bind precomputed, per-item JSON-LD into each template's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> — covers every qualifying page automatically but is more setup work and a structural CMS change; or (2) hand-build static JSON-LD for a handful of the highest-traffic treatment pages only — faster to ship, doesn't scale. Held off pending that decision; the filtered 172-question list is ready to use whichever path is chosen. Any future implementation should be validated against <a href="https://validator.schema.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">validator.schema.org</a> before publishing.</div></div></div>
    
    
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
