<div class="tab-panel" id="panel-h-hierarchy"><div class="sumcard good"><div class="sumcard-icon">🏗️</div><div class="sumcard-body"><div class="sumcard-head"><span class="pill good">Fixed — 22 July 2026</span><strong>Duplicate H1/H2 tags — resolved</strong></div><div class="sumcard-text">10 of 11 identified duplicate-heading root causes were fixed earlier: homepage sections, the shared reviews/footer/form/thank-you components, every CMS template's shared FAQ/result components, static pages, the Smile Gallery card titles, and 6 hand-typed duplicate/skipped headings found inside CMS RichText body fields (scoped across 428 EN items) were each retagged so the fix cascades to every page using that component. 5 of the report's original 11 duplicate-<em>H1</em> groups specifically (a related but distinct finding from the H2 root causes above) have also been resolved via distinct rewording drawn from each page's own existing content — Smile Makeovers gallery, Facial Aesthetics gallery, London Cosmetic Dentist, the Mayfair dentist sub-page, and one resolved by archiving a genuinely mismatched blog post (Palateless Dentures, whose body content belonged to a different post entirely). <strong>Social Six Consultation content-binding issue resolved (21 July 2026):</strong> the previously-undiagnosed mid-page section on <code>/teeth-veneers/porcelain-veneers/consultation-social-six</code> was traced to a static (not CMS-bound) H2 + paragraph reading "Why you'll want to consider Emax veneers as an option" — content that belongs to a completely different page and has no equivalent anywhere on the original WordPress source for this URL (checked directly). Since there's no real content to restore it to, the section was hidden (not deleted, nothing invented) and republished — confirmed live, no longer rendering. Of the 1 remaining H2 root cause: <code>/london-dentists</code> and <code>/dental-implant-brands</code> are genuine empty content stubs with no real copy to promote into a missing H2 — correctly left unfixed rather than inventing content. <strong>The remaining 6 duplicate-H1 groups were re-verified live, fresh, on 22 July 2026 (not assumed from the original list)</strong>: 3 were confirmed as genuine duplicates and fixed by rewording the lower-priority page's H1 using text already present in that page's own meta-title/title field — <code>/blog/dental-implants-common-questions</code> ("Dental Implant FAQs" &rarr; "Your Dental Implant Questions Answered"), <code>/blog/dental-implants-surgery-cost</code> ("Dental Implant Costs" &rarr; "Is a Dental Implant Worth the Cost?"), and the static <code>/dental-bridge</code> page ("Dental Bridges" &rarr; "Dental Bridge in London") — all 3 verified live. The remaining 3 from the original list did not hold up under fresh verification: "Dentures" no longer exists as a duplicate (the client independently restructured <code>/general-dentists/dentures</code> to redirect to <code>/dentures</code> since the original finding); "Professional Whitening" and "All-on-4 Implants" could not be matched to an actual live duplicate H1 pair — the originally-cited page combinations do not share identical H1 text on the current live site. <strong>0 known duplicate-H1 pairs remain.</strong></div></div></div><div class="sumcard partial"><div class="sumcard-icon">🏗️</div><div class="sumcard-body"><div class="sumcard-head"><span class="pill partial">New findings — 25 Jul 2026 live verification</span><strong>1 duplicate-H1 group, ~88 duplicate-H2 groups found (mostly low-severity template pattern)</strong></div><div class="sumcard-text">Fresh full re-crawl of all 549 EN pages. <strong>Duplicate H1 text:</strong> "All on 4 Dental Implants" is the H1 on 3 different pages — <code>/blog/all-on-4</code>, <code>/blog/all-on-4-dental-implants</code>, <code>/dentalimplant/all-on-4</code> — verified these are 3 genuinely different pages with different titles/body content (not the June mass-duplicate-post issue, already confirmed cleaned up), so this is same-wording-different-content overlap, not true duplication. Worth a rewording pass on the lower-priority page(s). <strong>Duplicate H2 text:</strong> 93 groups found; 5 are already-known sitewide boilerplate (Come visit us / New Patient Registration / Thank You! / FAQ / Meet the team). Of the remaining 88, most are shared section headers repeating by design across the London-borough × treatment-type template set and the Team bio template (e.g. "Patient review", "Education and credentials", "Timeline") — low severity, expected for this page architecture. A handful of more topically meaningful repeats worth a light differentiation pass: "Your Implant Questions Answered" (3×), "What are dental implants?" (3×), "When Should You Get a Second Opinion?" (2×). Full list of all 88 groups: <code>scratchpad/audit_2026-07-25/fresh_analysis.json</code>.</div></div></div><div class="sumcard done"><div class="sumcard-icon">🏗️</div><div class="sumcard-body"><div class="sumcard-head"><span class="pill done">Fixed — 25 Jul 2026</span><strong>4 of 5 meaningful duplicate-H2 groups resolved</strong></div><div class="sumcard-text">Fixed via rewording using each page's own existing content (no invented copy): "Your Implant Questions Answered" (kept canonical on dental-implants-common-questions, reworded dental-implants-qa's H2 to "Dental Implants Q&A" matching its own title). "What are dental implants?" (kept canonical on dental-implants-vs-removable-dentures, reworded dental-implants-what-you-should-expect's H2 to match its own title). "When Should You Get a Second Opinion?" (kept canonical on /second-opinion/dental-implant, reworded /second-opinion/denture to "Signs You May Need a Denture Second Opinion"). "Can Failed Implants from Turkey Be Saved?" (kept canonical on the CMS-templated /complex-dental-cases/dental-implant, reworded the static /failing-dental-implant page to "Fixing Dental Implants Placed in Turkey"). All published and live. <strong>Not fixed:</strong> "Final Thoughts" (2 unidentified blog posts, low priority — an extremely generic, common blog-conclusion heading, not pursued further given the cost of locating them in a 397-item collection). The other 83 boilerplate groups remain intentionally untouched (shared template sections, correct by design).</div></div></div>
