<p class="subtitle" style="margin-bottom:16px;">Every one of the site's 422 tracked WordPress-mapped URLs was live-fetched on the production domain (maidasmiles.co.uk) after go-live and checked for: which system currently serves the page, title tag presence/relevance, meta description presence/relevance, canonical correctness, accidental noindex, content-topic accuracy, broken internal links, pricing/data consistency, and visible typos.</p>

<div class="sumcard info">
<div class="sumcard-icon">ℹ️</div>
<div class="sumcard-body">
<div class="sumcard-head"><span class="pill info">Note on URL structure</span><strong>Same-looking URLs are expected, not a redirect failure</strong></div>
<div class="sumcard-text">Many pages intentionally kept the exact same URL path as the old WordPress site to preserve their existing SEO equity — that part is by design and is <strong>not</strong> flagged as an issue anywhere in this report. What we checked instead is which system is actually rendering each of those URLs today and whether the content on it is correct. The distinction that matters for SEO is not "did the path change" but "is the page live, correctly matched to its topic, and free of broken links/data errors" — that's what the table below covers.</div>
</div>
</div>

